        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "Andreas Dilger",
          "email": "adilger@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-10-06 00:43:51.000000000",
          "tz": -360
        },
        "subject": "LU-16678 obdclass: limit QoS weight by bytes per inode",
        "message": "LU-16678 obdclass: limit QoS weight by bytes per inode\n\nThe QoS weight of an OST is only its free space, so an OST holding\nmany small objects gets new objects until it runs out of inodes, while\nthe weight of an MDT multiplies its free space and inodes, so free\nspace the MDT can't use for lack of inodes still raises its weight.\n\nAdd lu_tgt_qos_avail() to count the free space and free inodes of a\ntarget only as far as the other can use them, with a factor of 2\nslack each way, at the free space per inode of the empty target.\nOnly ldiskfs targets have a fixed number of inodes to limit this way.\nUse it for the weights in lu_tgt_qos_weight_calc(), and for the\npenalties and the choice between round-robin and QoS allocation in\nltd_qos_penalties_calc() and lod_pool_qos_penalties_calc().  An OST\nformatted with 1 MiB per inode, with 1 TiB and 100K inodes free, then\ncounts as 200 GiB free.  The bytes per inode used so far would reflect\nthe objects better, but precreated empty objects skew it enough on a\nnew OST to invert the weights.  Also recalculate the weights when only\nthe free inodes of a target change.\n\nAdd sanity test_51k to check that an OST with most of its inodes used\nby empty objects gets a lower QoS weight.\n\nSigned-off-by: Andreas Dilger <adilger@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5.5 ltvm,llm_code_and_review_tools\nChange-Id: I6befde59003074bd0a1854de10cfa99a963ebbe5\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
